      "source_reference": "Ace of English - VSTEP Test Library (Reading Skills)",
      "content": "Since the advent of the Industrial Revolution, human activities—principally the combustion of fossil fuels, cement manufacturing, and widespread deforestation—have released hundreds of billions of metric tons of carbon dioxide into the Earth's atmosphere. While the atmospheric accumulation of greenhouse gases has fueled global warming, the world's oceans have acted as a massive buffer, absorbing roughly 30 percent of all anthropogenic carbon dioxide emissions. Without this substantial marine sink, atmospheric temperatures would have escalated even more catastrophically. However, this vital oceanic buffering comes at a severe ecological price: a fundamental alteration of seawater chemistry known as ocean acidification.\n\nWhen carbon dioxide dissolves in water, it initiates a series of chemical reactions forming carbonic acid. This acid dissociates into hydrogen ions and bicarbonate ions, which in turn causes the concentration of free hydrogen ions to increase, thereby lowering the pH of the ocean. Since the late eighteenth century, the average surface ocean pH has declined from 8.2 to 8.1. Because the pH scale is logarithmic, this seemingly modest drop of 0.1 units represents an alarming 30 percent increase in acidity. Projections suggest that unless carbon emissions drop precipitously, surface seawater acidity could rise by more than 100 percent by the end of this century.\n\nThe biological implications of this shift are profound, particularly for calcifying organisms. Creatures such as corals, clams, oysters, sea urchins, and tiny pelagic snails known as pteropods rely on ambient carbonate ions to construct their protective shells and calcium carbonate skeletons. As excess hydrogen ions bind with available carbonate ions, they drastically reduce the saturation horizon of carbonate minerals. In severely acidified waters, calcifying organisms must expend excessive metabolic energy simply to maintain their shells, leaving diminished resources for growth, reproduction, and disease defense. Under extreme conditions, thin calcium carbonate shells can literally begin to dissolve.\n\nThe vulnerability of pteropods is of paramount concern to marine biologists. Often referred to as 'sea butterflies,' these diminutive swimming snails form the bedrock of polar and subpolar marine food webs, serving as a primary dietary staple for salmon, herring, seabirds, and baleen whales. Laboratory studies indicate that pteropod shells exposed to anticipated future ocean conditions dissolve within weeks. The collapse of pteropod populations could trigger devastating trophic cascades throughout oceanic ecosystems, threatening not only commercial fisheries but global food security for coastal human populations.",
